  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The District of Columbia has $30.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal buying community issued 42,020 awards, with an average award size of $723K, and obligations are down 3.3% year over year. The Department of Defense leads buying with $5.7B, followed by the General Services Administration with $4.2B and the Department of Transportation with $4B. BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC and DELOITTE CONSULTING LLP are the top vendors with $1.2B and $1.1B respectively, and HARRIS CORPORATION follows with $901M. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry by obligations at $4.3B, with Engineering Services and Other Computer Related Services close behind at $3.8B and $3.7B. Fiscal-year comparisons show obligations peaked in FY24 at $36B and have declined since, with FY25 at $31.4B and FY26 to date at $30.4B. This report highlights where federal dollars are concentrated by agency, vendor, and NAICS industry in the District of Columbia for the current fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year in District of Columbia federal contract obligations?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 3.3% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show a high point of $36B in FY24, then a decline to $31.4B in FY25 and to $30.4B in FY26 to date. The number of awards and the average award size combine to produce the current-year total of $30.4B across 42,020 awards at an average of $723K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries drive federal spending in the District of Columbia?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer at $5.7B, followed by the General Services Administration at $4.2B and the Department of Transportation at $4B. Computer Systems Design Services leads industries with $4.3B, with Engineering Services at $3.8B and Other Computer Related Services at $3.7B. These agencies and industries together account for a significant share of the $30.4B in obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
